    <!-- ================= ENTIRE ================= -->
    <section>
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div class="eyebrow"><span class="swatch" style="background:var(--entire)"></span>Tool one</div>
        <h2>Entire</h2>
        <p class="tagline">“The git forge for AI-native teams” · Go · MIT · <span class="dmono">entire.io</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p>Git records the diff and throws away the argument. When a human wrote the code, the commit message carried some of the reasoning; when an agent wrote it, the prompt, the three approaches it rejected, and the tool calls it made all evaporate the moment the session closes. Entire's bet is that this lost context is the most valuable thing an AI-native repo produces — so it captures the session and stores it <em>inside git itself</em>, keyed to the commit.</p>
        <p>It is also, separately, a full distributed git host: regional mirrors of your GitHub repos in US East, EU Central and Australia, with published throughput figures of ~570k clones/hour and ~2.1M pushes/hour at 50–60ms median latency. For a 48-hour hackathon you can ignore all of that and use only the capture layer on top of your existing GitHub remote.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>How capture actually works</h3>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 452" role="img" aria-label="Flow: a coding agent session is captured by agent hooks into a local uncommitted session store. Running git commit fires a post-commit hook that redacts secrets on device, then writes a checkpoint. The checkpoint splits into a git ref under refs slash entire slash checkpoints and a commit trailer. The ref is pushed to the entire.io mirror and read back through the CLI and MCP.">
