      <div class="flat prose">
        <div class="eyebrow">Privacy, stated plainly</div>
        <p class="note">Session transcripts live in your repository's git objects. <strong>If the repo is public, so are they.</strong> Entire redacts detected secrets, API keys, tokens and credentials before a checkpoint is created, with optional PII and OpenAI-privacy-filter passes — but the docs call redaction <em>best-effort</em>. The hackathon requires a public repo, so don't paste a live Stripe key into a prompt you intend to commit. <code>--checkpoint-remote</code> can send checkpoints to a separate private repo if you want the code public and the transcripts not.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Graph and Brain are not the same thing</h3>

      <div class="prose stack">
        <p>These are the two pieces most teams conflate, and the Entire prize is specifically for creative use of Checkpoints, <em>Graph</em> or <em>Brain</em>. The split is clean: Graph reads your source code and tells you how it fits together. Brain reads everything else — sessions, facts, docs, plus Graph's output — and tells you why it is that way.</p>
      </div>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 880 330" role="img" aria-label="Two layers. Graph: the source tree is parsed locally by tree-sitter into an index that answers ranked search, definitions, callers and blast radius. Brain: sessions, checkpoints, durable facts and documents, plus Graph's code structure, are indexed and queried with BM25 plus vector search fused by reciprocal rank fusion, answering why decisions were made and how to approach similar work.">
